00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,263
Over the course of an fMRI run, heating in
the magnet gradients and subtle

2
00:00:06,263 --> 00:00:10,289
participant physiological shifts cause
slow, monotonic changes in baseline

3
00:00:10,289 --> 00:00:10,737
signal.

4
00:00:10,737 --> 00:00:15,658
High-pass temporal filtering removes these
low-frequency trends so they do not

5
00:00:15,658 --> 00:00:17,000
contaminate experimental regressors.

6
00:00:21,567 --> 00:00:25,146
Setting the high-pass filter cutoff
requires balancing drift removal against

7
00:00:25,146 --> 00:00:26,220
preserving task signal.

8
00:00:26,220 --> 00:00:31,589
The golden rule is: cutoff must be at
least twice the duration of the longest

9
00:00:31,589 --> 00:00:32,305
experimental cycle.

10
00:00:32,305 --> 00:00:38,031
For a block design with thirty seconds on
and thirty seconds off, the cycle is sixty

11
00:00:38,031 --> 00:00:42,327
seconds, requiring a cutoff of at least
one hundred and twenty seconds.

12
00:00:47,933 --> 00:00:52,351
If you set your cutoff too short, the
filter cannot distinguish scanner drift

13
00:00:52,351 --> 00:00:54,729
from the hemodynamic response of your
task.

14
00:00:54,729 --> 00:00:57,448
It will filter out your experimental
effect entirely.

15
00:00:57,448 --> 00:01:02,884
When in doubt, err on the side of a longer
cutoff; residual drift can still be

16
00:01:02,884 --> 00:01:04,583
captured by linear trend regressors.

17
00:01:09,300 --> 00:01:12,598
High-pass temporal filtering removes
scanner drift while preserving

18
00:01:12,598 --> 00:01:13,834
experimental BOLD signal.

19
00:01:13,834 --> 00:01:19,193
Always calculate your cutoff from the
longest task cycle, and verify that the

20
00:01:19,193 --> 00:01:22,490
design matrix in FEAT shows no filter
attenuation.
